{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TRFLmHke27aggqytkHRACV","type":"tool_result","content":"FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS = {\"success\", \"neutral\", \"skipped\"}\n\n# issue #84: a concluded check's second token is a conclusion --\n# FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS, or another concluded one such as failure,\n# cancelled, or timed_out; an unconcluded check's is a status instead,\n# one of these. drive's own poll (poll_forge_checks) is the one reader\n# that needs to tell a still-running check apart from a concluded, not-ok\n# one -- read_forge_checks itself only ever needed \"green or not\".\nFORGE_UNCONCLUDED_STATUSES = {\"queued\", \"in_progress\", \"pending\", \"waiting\"}\n\n\ndef read_forge_checks(root: Path, checks_command: str, sha: str) -> tuple[dict[str, Any], bool]:\n    \"\"\"Runs the tenant's [forge].checks_command on sha; the product does\n    not know what forge that command reads. One line per check as\n    \"<name> <conclusion>\"; a command that fails to run, or any conclusion\n    other than success/neutral/skipped, is not green.\"\"\"\n    run = subprocess.run(f\"{checks_command} {shlex.quote(sha)}\", shell=True, cwd=str(root),\n                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n    conclusions: list[dict[str, str]] = []\n    for line in run.stdout.splitlines():\n        line = line.strip()\n        if not line:\n            continue\n        parts = line.rsplit(\" \", 1)\n        if len(parts) == 2:\n            conclusions.append({\"name\": parts[0], \"conclusion\": parts[1]})\n    # A commit the forge has no record of is not green -- it is unread.\n    # The executor's own commit is exactly this: the forge attaches\n    # checks to a pull request's head, and that commit stops being the\n    # head the moment a record commit lands on top of it. all() over an\n    # empty list is True, which is why this is spelled out rather than\n    # implied by the loop above.\n    ok = run.returncode == 0 and bool(conclusions) and all(\n        c[\"conclusion\"] in FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS for c in conclusions)\n    return {\"command\": checks_command, \"sha\": sha, \"conclusions\": conclusions}, ok\n    \"\"\"Whether poll_forge_checks should keep waiting rather than stop at\n    once: an empty read (the forge has not attached any check to this sha\n    yet) or any one check still carrying a status (queued, in_progress,\n    pending, waiting) rather than a concluded conclusion. A concluded\n    conclusion that is not green (failure, cancelled, timed_out, and\n    anything else read_forge_checks did not recognize as ok) is not this\n    -- that check is done, whether or not it passed.\"\"\"\n    conclusions = forge_checks[\"conclusions\"]\n    if not conclusions:\n        return True\n    return any(c[\"conclusion\"] in FORGE_UNCONCLUDED_STATUSES for c in conclusions)\n\n\ndef poll_forge_checks(root: Path, checks_command: str, sha: str) -> tuple[dict[str, Any], bool]:\n    \"\"\"read_forge_checks, retried within drive_forge_poll_bound_s() while\n    some check is still unconcluded (forge_checks_unconcluded) -- a check\n    still running is not yet red, and drive gives it a little room to\n    conclude before it gives up. A read where every check has already\n    concluded, green or not, stops here at once: there is nothing left to\n    wait on. The bound and interval are constants this lane names, not a\n    manifest key, re-read at call time so a test's own clock\n    (ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_BOUND_S, ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_INTERVAL_S) never has to\n    survive past when it was set.\"\"\"\n    bound = drive_forge_poll_bound_s()\n    interval = drive_forge_poll_interval_s()\n    deadline = time.monotonic() + bound\n    forge_checks, ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, sha)\n    while not ok and forge_checks_unconcluded(forge_checks) and time.monotonic() < deadline:\n        time.sleep(interval)\n        forge_checks, ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, sha)\n    return forge_checks, ok\n                   else default_remote_ref)\n    try:\n        out = git(root, \"log\", default_ref, \"--first-parent\", \"--merges\",\n                 \"--diff-merges=first-parent\", \"--name-only\",\n                 \"--format=\\x01%H\\x1f%P\")\n    except RuntimeError:\n        return [], None\n    merges = []\n    for block in out.split(\"\\x01\")[1:]:\n        header, _, rest = block.partition(\"\\n\")\n        sha, parents = header.split(\"\\x1f\")\n        merges.append({\"sha\": sha, \"parents\": parents.split(),\n                       \"touched\": [line for line in rest.splitlines() if line.strip()]})\n    return merges, None\n\n\ndef merge_introducing_records(root: Path, records_rel: str, item: str,\n                              stages: tuple[str, ...],\n                              merges: list[dict[str, Any]] | None = None) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"(merge_sha, refusal): the merge commit on the default branch's own\n    first-parent line whose first-parent diff introduces a record for\n    every one of stages, under records_rel/item/ -- the same derivation\n    attest itself uses (derive_attest_items), read here so proof two\n    requires an item's stage records to have arrived by an actual merge\n    on the default branch, not merely to sit in the tree, and not by a\n    transport merge, a pull_request checkout's synthetic test merge, or a\n    merge_group's temporary merge (issue #69 acceptance row 4; issue\n    #169). merge_sha is None with no refusal when no such merge exists\n    yet -- the item is not merged, not that the walk failed.\n\n    merges, when given, is _merges_reachable's own result -- ledger's\n    cmd_ledger computes it once and passes it to every item rather than\n    walking the default branch's merges again for each (issue #153); a\n    caller that already has merges has also already handled\n    _merges_reachable's refusal, so this never re-derives one. Left\n    None, a caller with just the one item to ask (doctor's proof two)\n    gets merges computed here, refusal and all.\"\"\"\n    if merges is None:\n        merges, refusal = _merges_reachable(root)\n        if refusal:\n            return None, refusal\n    prefix = f\"{records_rel.rstrip('/')}/{item}/\"\n    for merge in merges:\n        found: set[str] = set()\n        for p in merge[\"touched\"]:\n            if p.startswith(prefix):\n                m = RECORD_RE.match(Path(p).name)\n                if m:\n                    found.add(m.group(1))\n        if all(s in found for s in stages):\n            return merge[\"sha\"], None\n    return None, None\n\n\nINVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX = \".started\"\n\n\ndef invocation_marker_path(records_dir: Path, stage: str, nn: int) -> Path:\n    return records_dir / f\"{stage}-{nn:02d}{INVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX}\"\n\n\ndef write_invocation_marker(root: Path, records_dir: Path, stage: str, nn: int) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"The fact that an invocation began, written before it does: at,\n    host, pid, the sha it starts on, and the product that started it.\n    Its record's arrival is what retires it; a marker with no record is\n    an invocation that died or hangs, and proof five names it.\"\"\"\n    import os\n    import socket\n    from datetime import datetime, timezone\n    records_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    path = invocation_marker_path(records_dir, stage, nn)\n    sha = git(root, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\").strip()\n    info = {\"at\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\"),\n            \"host\": socket.gethostname(), \"pid\": os.getpid(), \"sha\": sha,\n            \"product_version\": product_version()}\n    path.write_text(json.dumps(info, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n    return path\n\n\ndef invocation_alive(info: dict[str, Any]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether the marker's pid is a live process on this host. Another\n    host's pid is unknowable from here and reads as not alive: a drive\n    that ran elsewhere and wrote no record is died by absence.\"\"\"\n    import os\n    import socket\n    if info.get(\"host\") != socket.gethostname():\n        return False\n    pid = info.get(\"pid\")\n    if not isinstance(pid, int) or isinstance(pid, bool) or pid <= 0:\n        return False\n    try:\n        os.kill(pid, 0)\n    except ProcessLookupError:\n        return False\n    except PermissionError:\n        return True\n    return True\n\n\ndef invocation_age_s(info: dict[str, Any]) -> float | None:\n    \"\"\"Seconds since the marker's own `at` timestamp -- how long ago the\n    invocation it names began, whatever host wrote it. None when `at` is\n    missing or does not parse.\"\"\"\n    from datetime import datetime, timezone\n    at = info.get(\"at\")\n    if not isinstance(at, str):\n        return None\n    try:\n        stamp = datetime.strptime(at, \"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\").replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n    return (datetime.now(timezone.utc) - stamp).total_seconds()\n\n\ndef invocations_without_record(item_records_dir: Path) -> list[tuple[Path, dict[str, Any]]]:\n    \"\"\"Every <stage>-NN.started under one item whose <stage>-NN.json has\n    not arrived, with the marker's own dict ({} when unreadable).\"\"\"\n    out: list[tuple[Path, dict[str, Any]]] = []\n    if not item_records_dir.is_dir():\n        return out\n    for marker in sorted(item_records_dir.glob(f\"*{INVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX}\")):\n        if (item_records_dir / f\"{marker.stem}.json\").is_file():\n            continue\n        try:\n            with marker.open() as fh:\n                info = json.load(fh)\n        except (OSError, ValueError):\n            info = {}\n        out.append((marker, info if isinstance(info, dict) else {}))\n    return out\n\n\ndef stall_bound_s(m: dict[str, Any]) -> int | None:\n    \"\"\"[drive].stall_after_s: the tenant's own bound past which a live\n    invocation with no new event is hung. A bound the tenant did not\n    write is a forecast, so there is no default.\"\"\"\n    bound = (m.get(\"drive\") or {}).get(\"stall_after_s\")\n    if isinstance(bound, bool) or not isinstance(bound, (int, float)) or bound <= 0:\n        return None\n    return int(bound)\n\n\ndef proof_no_invocation_lacks_record(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any] | None) -> Proof:\n    import socket\n    p = Proof(\"no-invocation-lacks-record\", \"no invocation lacks its record\")\n    if m is None:\n        return p.fail(f\"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; no records directory is declared\")\n    records_rel = m.get(\"paths\", {}).get(\"records\", \"\")\n    records_dir = root / records_rel\n    if not records_dir.is_dir():\n        return p.fail(f\"no records directory under {records_dir}\")\n    bound = stall_bound_s(m)\n    if bound is None:\n        return p.fail(\"no [drive].stall_after_s in the manifest; a hung invocation cannot be \"\n                      \"told from a running one\")\n    this_host = socket.gethostname()\n    recorded = 0\n    running: list[str] = []\n    in_flight: list[str] = []\n    problems: list[str] = []\n    for item_dir in sorted(d for d in records_dir.iterdir() if d.is_dir()):\n    elif latest_spec.get(\"spec_sha\") != spec_sha_now:\n        refusal = (f\"spec-gate record is stale: recorded {latest_spec.get('spec_sha')}, \"\n                   f\"spec now at {spec_sha_now}\")\n    elif by not in approvers:\n        refusal = f\"{by} is not in [operators].approvers\"\n\n    if refusal:\n        print(f\"attavo approve: refused: {refusal}\")\n        return 1\n\n    record = {\"sha\": git(root, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\").strip(), \"spec_sha\": spec_sha_now,\n              \"approved_by\": by, \"at\": now_iso()}\n    write_record(records_dir, \"approval\", record, m, item_id)\n    print(f\"attavo approve: item {item_id} approved by {by}\")\n    return 0\n\n\ndef approval_status(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any], item_id: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\"absent\", \"expired\", or \"valid\": whether the latest approval record\n    for item_id still binds to the spec's current commit. Raises\n    ShallowCheckoutError on a shallow checkout, same as last_commit_touching.\n    Shared by cmd_approval_status and cmd_build so build refuses on exactly\n    what `attavo approval-status` would print.\n    \"\"\"\n    specs_rel = m[\"paths\"][\"specs\"]\n    records_rel = m[\"paths\"][\"records\"]\n    spec_rel = str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id)\n    spec_sha_now = last_commit_touching(root, spec_rel)\n    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id\n    latest_approval = latest_record(records_dir, \"approval\")\n\n    if latest_approval is None:\n        return \"absent\"\n    if latest_approval.get(\"spec_sha\") == spec_sha_now:\n        return \"valid\"\n    return \"expired\"\n\n\ndef cmd_approval_status(argv: list[str]) -> int:\n    root = Path(\".\").resolve()\n    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)\n    if not item_id:\n        print(\"attavo approval-status: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch\",\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    m = read_manifest(root)\n    if m is None:\n        print(f\"attavo approval-status: no {MANIFEST} at {root}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    try:\n        status = approval_status(root, m, item_id)\n    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:\n        print(f\"attavo approval-status: refused: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    print(status)\n    return 0 if status == \"valid\" else 1\n\n\n# The typed loaders: a literal stage name selects the record kind the caller\n# gets back (issue #189). The record is read from a file and not validated\n# here, so the kind is the schema's promise, the same one the write side\n# already keeps (write_record refuses a key its schema does not declare);\n# a stage held in a variable is the untyped fallback.\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"spec\"]) -> dict[int, SpecRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"approval\"]) -> dict[int, ApprovalRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"build\"]) -> dict[int, BuildRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"verify\"]) -> dict[int, VerifyRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"counsel\"]) -> dict[int, CounselRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict[int, Any]: ...\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict[int, Any]:\n    attempts: dict[int, Any] = {}\n    if not records_dir.is_dir():\n        return attempts\n    for f in records_dir.glob(f\"{stage}-*.json\"):\n        m = RECORD_RE.match(f.name)\n        if not m or m.group(1) != stage:\n            continue\n        try:\n            attempts[int(m.group(2))] = _load_record(f, records_dir.name)\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            continue\n    return attempts\n\n\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"spec\"]) -> SpecRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"approval\"]) -> ApprovalRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"build\"]) -> BuildRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"verify\"]) -> VerifyRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"counsel\"]) -> CounselRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Any | None: ...\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Any:\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    return attempts[max(attempts)] if attempts else None\n\n\ndef next_attempt_number(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> int:\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    return (max(attempts) + 1) if attempts else 1\n\n\ndef latest_attempt_path(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Path | None:\n    \"\"\"The path of the highest-numbered <stage>-NN.json, or None.\"\"\"\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    if not attempts:\n        return None\n    return records_dir / f\"{stage}-{max(attempts):02d}.json\"\n\n\ndef write_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str, record: Mapping[str, Any], m: dict[str, Any],\n                 item: str, log: str | None = None) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"Every record gains record_version (this stage schema's own $id\n    version -- issue #60) and item (verbatim from the caller, matching the\n    record's directory segment), plus product_version (what ran, derived\n    here) and product_pin (what the tenant declared, copied verbatim from\n    the manifest, or null when the manifest carries no [attavo] table) --\n\"\"\"attavo: the command.\n\n    attavo doctor [TENANT_ROOT] [--json]\n    attavo spec-gate [N]\n    attavo approve [N] --by LOGIN\n    attavo approval-status [N]\n    attavo build [N]\n    attavo verify [N]\n    attavo file-findings [N]\n    attavo file-stops [N]\n    attavo counsel [N]\n    attavo record-only\n    attavo attest [--partial] [N]\n    attavo drive [N] [--until-ready]\n    attavo ledger [N] [--json]\n    attavo stops [N] [--json]\n\nThe doctor is the definition of done. It asserts five proofs against a\ntenant root and exits 1 until every proof holds:\n\n  1. every-gate-rejects      every gate in the manifest's [gates] table,\n                             run against its rejection fixture, exits non-zero,\n                             and carries a non-empty retires\n  2. one-item-every-stage    one work item under paths.records carries a\n                             record for every stage -- spec, approval,\nprinted) rather than a refusal, while the two chain assertions -- build's\napproval_sha names the latest approval, verify's build_sha names the\nlatest build -- still run over whatever records exist. merge_group and a\npush to main still demand the whole chain. See issue #69's lane for the\nfull rules it enforces.\n\ndrive reads an item's latest records and a forge's own check conclusions\nand performs the next mechanical step, and only that step, then stops: no\nvalid approval refuses by name and runs nothing; no passing build bound to\nthat approval runs build; a passing build with the forge green on the\nbranch head (polled for a bound, not the manifest) runs verify; a passing\nverify with the forge still green prints that the item is ready to merge\nand stops there -- the merge is never drive's own act, and it enqueues\nnothing. A record build or verify writes that is not pass is counseled (counsel,\nissue #100) and filed (file-stops, issue #89) before it is transported,\nso its .filed marker exists in time to be committed alongside it; drive\nthen commits the stage record and the counsel record together -- each\nwith its own log and marker -- with `git commit --only`, never sweeping\nthe index, and pushes; a push a divergent remote rejects\nis retried once after `git pull --no-rebase --no-edit`, never a rebase,\nsince the record already names the commit's sha. drive writes no record\nof its own: a second run in the same state repeats the same step or the\nsame stop, reading exactly what the prior run left behind -- the marker\nincluded, so a stop already filed is never filed again, from this\nworktree or a fresh one. See issue #84's lane for the full rules it\nenforces.\n\n--until-ready (issue #100's own followups) repeats step-and-wait --\nstep, the forge poll already inside a step, step again -- in the same\nprocess, rather than making the caller invoke drive again after every\npassing step, until the item is ready to merge or a step stops on its\nown account (a refusal, a halted or failed or refused record, or the\nforge never going green); a step that stops still prints exactly what\nthe same step would print without the flag. Without --until-ready,\ndrive is unchanged: one step, then stop, exactly as before.\n\nledger reads, never writes. For item N (or every item under\npaths.records when N is omitted), it walks the commits from the item's\nfirst spec commit to its merge (the same derivation doctor's proof two\nand attest use) or to HEAD when the item has not merged, and prints one\nrow per act: the sha, the identity as git names it, and a class from a\nclosed list -- spec, amend, approve, launch, record, change, transport,\nmerge -- derived from who committed it and, for an approval, build, or\nverify record, what the record itself says. A summary line follows:\noperator acts (approve, amend, launch, transport, merge), product acts\n(record), executor commits (change), stops (non-pass records), and\nsince (the product version on the first record). A build or verify\nrecord's own commit, from before v0.18.0 gave the driver and the\nexecutor their own identities, prints as record-or-launch with a note,\ncounted toward neither total, rather than guessed. --json prints the\nrows and the summary as one object. See issue #128's lane for the full","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8de61c42-af71-4210-8ec4-d6e4cd3c023f","uuid":"2a9fe00c-e7fb-401a-9bcc-51800fc06f0a","timestamp":"2026-09-20T17:33:04.287Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS = {\"success\", \"neutral\", \"skipped\"}\n\n# issue #84: a concluded check's second token is a conclusion --\n# FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS, or another concluded one such as failure,\n# cancelled, or timed_out; an unconcluded check's is a status instead,\n# one of these. drive's own poll (poll_forge_checks) is the one reader\n# that needs to tell a still-running check apart from a concluded, not-ok\n# one -- read_forge_checks itself only ever needed \"green or not\".\nFORGE_UNCONCLUDED_STATUSES = {\"queued\", \"in_progress\", \"pending\", \"waiting\"}\n\n\ndef read_forge_checks(root: Path, checks_command: str, sha: str) -> tuple[dict[str, Any], bool]:\n    \"\"\"Runs the tenant's [forge].checks_command on sha; the product does\n    not know what forge that command reads. One line per check as\n    \"<name> <conclusion>\"; a command that fails to run, or any conclusion\n    other than success/neutral/skipped, is not green.\"\"\"\n    run = subprocess.run(f\"{checks_command} {shlex.quote(sha)}\", shell=True, cwd=str(root),\n                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n    conclusions: list[dict[str, str]] = []\n    for line in run.stdout.splitlines():\n        line = line.strip()\n        if not line:\n            continue\n        parts = line.rsplit(\" \", 1)\n        if len(parts) == 2:\n            conclusions.append({\"name\": parts[0], \"conclusion\": parts[1]})\n    # A commit the forge has no record of is not green -- it is unread.\n    # The executor's own commit is exactly this: the forge attaches\n    # checks to a pull request's head, and that commit stops being the\n    # head the moment a record commit lands on top of it. all() over an\n    # empty list is True, which is why this is spelled out rather than\n    # implied by the loop above.\n    ok = run.returncode == 0 and bool(conclusions) and all(\n        c[\"conclusion\"] in FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS for c in conclusions)\n    return {\"command\": checks_command, \"sha\": sha, \"conclusions\": conclusions}, ok\n    \"\"\"Whether poll_forge_checks should keep waiting rather than stop at\n    once: an empty read (the forge has not attached any check to this sha\n    yet) or any one check still carrying a status (queued, in_progress,\n    pending, waiting) rather than a concluded conclusion. A concluded\n    conclusion that is not green (failure, cancelled, timed_out, and\n    anything else read_forge_checks did not recognize as ok) is not this\n    -- that check is done, whether or not it passed.\"\"\"\n    conclusions = forge_checks[\"conclusions\"]\n    if not conclusions:\n        return True\n    return any(c[\"conclusion\"] in FORGE_UNCONCLUDED_STATUSES for c in conclusions)\n\n\ndef poll_forge_checks(root: Path, checks_command: str, sha: str) -> tuple[dict[str, Any], bool]:\n    \"\"\"read_forge_checks, retried within drive_forge_poll_bound_s() while\n    some check is still unconcluded (forge_checks_unconcluded) -- a check\n    still running is not yet red, and drive gives it a little room to\n    conclude before it gives up. A read where every check has already\n    concluded, green or not, stops here at once: there is nothing left to\n    wait on. The bound and interval are constants this lane names, not a\n    manifest key, re-read at call time so a test's own clock\n    (ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_BOUND_S, ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_INTERVAL_S) never has to\n    survive past when it was set.\"\"\"\n    bound = drive_forge_poll_bound_s()\n    interval = drive_forge_poll_interval_s()\n    deadline = time.monotonic() + bound\n    forge_checks, ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, sha)\n    while not ok and forge_checks_unconcluded(forge_checks) and time.monotonic() < deadline:\n        time.sleep(interval)\n        forge_checks, ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, sha)\n    return forge_checks, ok\n                   else default_remote_ref)\n    try:\n        out = git(root, \"log\", default_ref, \"--first-parent\", \"--merges\",\n                 \"--diff-merges=first-parent\", \"--name-only\",\n                 \"--format=\\x01%H\\x1f%P\")\n    except RuntimeError:\n        return [], None\n    merges = []\n    for block in out.split(\"\\x01\")[1:]:\n        header, _, rest = block.partition(\"\\n\")\n        sha, parents = header.split(\"\\x1f\")\n        merges.append({\"sha\": sha, \"parents\": parents.split(),\n                       \"touched\": [line for line in rest.splitlines() if line.strip()]})\n    return merges, None\n\n\ndef merge_introducing_records(root: Path, records_rel: str, item: str,\n                              stages: tuple[str, ...],\n                              merges: list[dict[str, Any]] | None = None) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"(merge_sha, refusal): the merge commit on the default branch's own\n    first-parent line whose first-parent diff introduces a record for\n    every one of stages, under records_rel/item/ -- the same derivation\n    attest itself uses (derive_attest_items), read here so proof two\n    requires an item's stage records to have arrived by an actual merge\n    on the default branch, not merely to sit in the tree, and not by a\n    transport merge, a pull_request checkout's synthetic test merge, or a\n    merge_group's temporary merge (issue #69 acceptance row 4; issue\n    #169). merge_sha is None with no refusal when no such merge exists\n    yet -- the item is not merged, not that the walk failed.\n\n    merges, when given, is _merges_reachable's own result -- ledger's\n    cmd_ledger computes it once and passes it to every item rather than\n    walking the default branch's merges again for each (issue #153); a\n    caller that already has merges has also already handled\n    _merges_reachable's refusal, so this never re-derives one. Left\n    None, a caller with just the one item to ask (doctor's proof two)\n    gets merges computed here, refusal and all.\"\"\"\n    if merges is None:\n        merges, refusal = _merges_reachable(root)\n        if refusal:\n            return None, refusal\n    prefix = f\"{records_rel.rstrip('/')}/{item}/\"\n    for merge in merges:\n        found: set[str] = set()\n        for p in merge[\"touched\"]:\n            if p.startswith(prefix):\n                m = RECORD_RE.match(Path(p).name)\n                if m:\n                    found.add(m.group(1))\n        if all(s in found for s in stages):\n            return merge[\"sha\"], None\n    return None, None\n\n\nINVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX = \".started\"\n\n\ndef invocation_marker_path(records_dir: Path, stage: str, nn: int) -> Path:\n    return records_dir / f\"{stage}-{nn:02d}{INVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX}\"\n\n\ndef write_invocation_marker(root: Path, records_dir: Path, stage: str, nn: int) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"The fact that an invocation began, written before it does: at,\n    host, pid, the sha it starts on, and the product that started it.\n    Its record's arrival is what retires it; a marker with no record is\n    an invocation that died or hangs, and proof five names it.\"\"\"\n    import os\n    import socket\n    from datetime import datetime, timezone\n    records_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    path = invocation_marker_path(records_dir, stage, nn)\n    sha = git(root, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\").strip()\n    info = {\"at\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\"),\n            \"host\": socket.gethostname(), \"pid\": os.getpid(), \"sha\": sha,\n            \"product_version\": product_version()}\n    path.write_text(json.dumps(info, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n    return path\n\n\ndef invocation_alive(info: dict[str, Any]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether the marker's pid is a live process on this host. Another\n    host's pid is unknowable from here and reads as not alive: a drive\n    that ran elsewhere and wrote no record is died by absence.\"\"\"\n    import os\n    import socket\n    if info.get(\"host\") != socket.gethostname():\n        return False\n    pid = info.get(\"pid\")\n    if not isinstance(pid, int) or isinstance(pid, bool) or pid <= 0:\n        return False\n    try:\n        os.kill(pid, 0)\n    except ProcessLookupError:\n        return False\n    except PermissionError:\n        return True\n    return True\n\n\ndef invocation_age_s(info: dict[str, Any]) -> float | None:\n    \"\"\"Seconds since the marker's own `at` timestamp -- how long ago the\n    invocation it names began, whatever host wrote it. None when `at` is\n    missing or does not parse.\"\"\"\n    from datetime import datetime, timezone\n    at = info.get(\"at\")\n    if not isinstance(at, str):\n        return None\n    try:\n        stamp = datetime.strptime(at, \"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\").replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n    return (datetime.now(timezone.utc) - stamp).total_seconds()\n\n\ndef invocations_without_record(item_records_dir: Path) -> list[tuple[Path, dict[str, Any]]]:\n    \"\"\"Every <stage>-NN.started under one item whose <stage>-NN.json has\n    not arrived, with the marker's own dict ({} when unreadable).\"\"\"\n    out: list[tuple[Path, dict[str, Any]]] = []\n    if not item_records_dir.is_dir():\n        return out\n    for marker in sorted(item_records_dir.glob(f\"*{INVOCATION_MARKER_SUFFIX}\")):\n        if (item_records_dir / f\"{marker.stem}.json\").is_file():\n            continue\n        try:\n            with marker.open() as fh:\n                info = json.load(fh)\n        except (OSError, ValueError):\n            info = {}\n        out.append((marker, info if isinstance(info, dict) else {}))\n    return out\n\n\ndef stall_bound_s(m: dict[str, Any]) -> int | None:\n    \"\"\"[drive].stall_after_s: the tenant's own bound past which a live\n    invocation with no new event is hung. A bound the tenant did not\n    write is a forecast, so there is no default.\"\"\"\n    bound = (m.get(\"drive\") or {}).get(\"stall_after_s\")\n    if isinstance(bound, bool) or not isinstance(bound, (int, float)) or bound <= 0:\n        return None\n    return int(bound)\n\n\ndef proof_no_invocation_lacks_record(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any] | None) -> Proof:\n    import socket\n    p = Proof(\"no-invocation-lacks-record\", \"no invocation lacks its record\")\n    if m is None:\n        return p.fail(f\"no {MANIFEST} at {root}; no records directory is declared\")\n    records_rel = m.get(\"paths\", {}).get(\"records\", \"\")\n    records_dir = root / records_rel\n    if not records_dir.is_dir():\n        return p.fail(f\"no records directory under {records_dir}\")\n    bound = stall_bound_s(m)\n    if bound is None:\n        return p.fail(\"no [drive].stall_after_s in the manifest; a hung invocation cannot be \"\n                      \"told from a running one\")\n    this_host = socket.gethostname()\n    recorded = 0\n    running: list[str] = []\n    in_flight: list[str] = []\n    problems: list[str] = []\n    for item_dir in sorted(d for d in records_dir.iterdir() if d.is_dir()):\n    elif latest_spec.get(\"spec_sha\") != spec_sha_now:\n        refusal = (f\"spec-gate record is stale: recorded {latest_spec.get('spec_sha')}, \"\n                   f\"spec now at {spec_sha_now}\")\n    elif by not in approvers:\n        refusal = f\"{by} is not in [operators].approvers\"\n\n    if refusal:\n        print(f\"attavo approve: refused: {refusal}\")\n        return 1\n\n    record = {\"sha\": git(root, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\").strip(), \"spec_sha\": spec_sha_now,\n              \"approved_by\": by, \"at\": now_iso()}\n    write_record(records_dir, \"approval\", record, m, item_id)\n    print(f\"attavo approve: item {item_id} approved by {by}\")\n    return 0\n\n\ndef approval_status(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any], item_id: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\"absent\", \"expired\", or \"valid\": whether the latest approval record\n    for item_id still binds to the spec's current commit. Raises\n    ShallowCheckoutError on a shallow checkout, same as last_commit_touching.\n    Shared by cmd_approval_status and cmd_build so build refuses on exactly\n    what `attavo approval-status` would print.\n    \"\"\"\n    specs_rel = m[\"paths\"][\"specs\"]\n    records_rel = m[\"paths\"][\"records\"]\n    spec_rel = str(Path(specs_rel) / item_id)\n    spec_sha_now = last_commit_touching(root, spec_rel)\n    records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id\n    latest_approval = latest_record(records_dir, \"approval\")\n\n    if latest_approval is None:\n        return \"absent\"\n    if latest_approval.get(\"spec_sha\") == spec_sha_now:\n        return \"valid\"\n    return \"expired\"\n\n\ndef cmd_approval_status(argv: list[str]) -> int:\n    root = Path(\".\").resolve()\n    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)\n    if not item_id:\n        print(\"attavo approval-status: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch\",\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    m = read_manifest(root)\n    if m is None:\n        print(f\"attavo approval-status: no {MANIFEST} at {root}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    try:\n        status = approval_status(root, m, item_id)\n    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:\n        print(f\"attavo approval-status: refused: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    print(status)\n    return 0 if status == \"valid\" else 1\n\n\n# The typed loaders: a literal stage name selects the record kind the caller\n# gets back (issue #189). The record is read from a file and not validated\n# here, so the kind is the schema's promise, the same one the write side\n# already keeps (write_record refuses a key its schema does not declare);\n# a stage held in a variable is the untyped fallback.\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"spec\"]) -> dict[int, SpecRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"approval\"]) -> dict[int, ApprovalRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"build\"]) -> dict[int, BuildRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"verify\"]) -> dict[int, VerifyRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"counsel\"]) -> dict[int, CounselRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict[int, Any]: ...\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict[int, Any]:\n    attempts: dict[int, Any] = {}\n    if not records_dir.is_dir():\n        return attempts\n    for f in records_dir.glob(f\"{stage}-*.json\"):\n        m = RECORD_RE.match(f.name)\n        if not m or m.group(1) != stage:\n            continue\n        try:\n            attempts[int(m.group(2))] = _load_record(f, records_dir.name)\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            continue\n    return attempts\n\n\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"spec\"]) -> SpecRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"approval\"]) -> ApprovalRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"build\"]) -> BuildRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"verify\"]) -> VerifyRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"counsel\"]) -> CounselRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Any | None: ...\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Any:\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    return attempts[max(attempts)] if attempts else None\n\n\ndef next_attempt_number(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> int:\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    return (max(attempts) + 1) if attempts else 1\n\n\ndef latest_attempt_path(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Path | None:\n    \"\"\"The path of the highest-numbered <stage>-NN.json, or None.\"\"\"\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    if not attempts:\n        return None\n    return records_dir / f\"{stage}-{max(attempts):02d}.json\"\n\n\ndef write_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str, record: Mapping[str, Any], m: dict[str, Any],\n                 item: str, log: str | None = None) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"Every record gains record_version (this stage schema's own $id\n    version -- issue #60) and item (verbatim from the caller, matching the\n    record's directory segment), plus product_version (what ran, derived\n    here) and product_pin (what the tenant declared, copied verbatim from\n    the manifest, or null when the manifest carries no [attavo] table) --\n\"\"\"attavo: the command.\n\n    attavo doctor [TENANT_ROOT] [--json]\n    attavo spec-gate [N]\n    attavo approve [N] --by LOGIN\n    attavo approval-status [N]\n    attavo build [N]\n    attavo verify [N]\n    attavo file-findings [N]\n    attavo file-stops [N]\n    attavo counsel [N]\n    attavo record-only\n    attavo attest [--partial] [N]\n    attavo drive [N] [--until-ready]\n    attavo ledger [N] [--json]\n    attavo stops [N] [--json]\n\nThe doctor is the definition of done. It asserts five proofs against a\ntenant root and exits 1 until every proof holds:\n\n  1. every-gate-rejects      every gate in the manifest's [gates] table,\n                             run against its rejection fixture, exits non-zero,\n                             and carries a non-empty retires\n  2. one-item-every-stage    one work item under paths.records carries a\n                             record for every stage -- spec, approval,\nprinted) rather than a refusal, while the two chain assertions -- build's\napproval_sha names the latest approval, verify's build_sha names the\nlatest build -- still run over whatever records exist. merge_group and a\npush to main still demand the whole chain. See issue #69's lane for the\nfull rules it enforces.\n\ndrive reads an item's latest records and a forge's own check conclusions\nand performs the next mechanical step, and only that step, then stops: no\nvalid approval refuses by name and runs nothing; no passing build bound to\nthat approval runs build; a passing build with the forge green on the\nbranch head (polled for a bound, not the manifest) runs verify; a passing\nverify with the forge still green prints that the item is ready to merge\nand stops there -- the merge is never drive's own act, and it enqueues\nnothing. A record build or verify writes that is not pass is counseled (counsel,\nissue #100) and filed (file-stops, issue #89) before it is transported,\nso its .filed marker exists in time to be committed alongside it; drive\nthen commits the stage record and the counsel record together -- each\nwith its own log and marker -- with `git commit --only`, never sweeping\nthe index, and pushes; a push a divergent remote rejects\nis retried once after `git pull --no-rebase --no-edit`, never a rebase,\nsince the record already names the commit's sha. drive writes no record\nof its own: a second run in the same state repeats the same step or the\nsame stop, reading exactly what the prior run left behind -- the marker\nincluded, so a stop already filed is never filed again, from this\nworktree or a fresh one. See issue #84's lane for the full rules it\nenforces.\n\n--until-ready (issue #100's own followups) repeats step-and-wait --\nstep, the forge poll already inside a step, step again -- in the same\nprocess, rather than making the caller invoke drive again after every\npassing step, until the item is ready to merge or a step stops on its\nown account (a refusal, a halted or failed or refused record, or the\nforge never going green); a step that stops still prints exactly what\nthe same step would print without the flag. Without --until-ready,\ndrive is unchanged: one step, then stop, exactly as before.\n\nledger reads, never writes. For item N (or every item under\npaths.records when N is omitted), it walks the commits from the item's\nfirst spec commit to its merge (the same derivation doctor's proof two\nand attest use) or to HEAD when the item has not merged, and prints one\nrow per act: the sha, the identity as git names it, and a class from a\nclosed list -- spec, amend, approve, launch, record, change, transport,\nmerge -- derived from who committed it and, for an approval, build, or\nverify record, what the record itself says. A summary line follows:\noperator acts (approve, amend, launch, transport, merge), product acts\n(record), executor commits (change), stops (non-pass records), and\nsince (the product version on the first record). A build or verify\nrecord's own commit, from before v0.18.0 gave the driver and the\nexecutor their own identities, prints as record-or-launch with a note,\ncounted toward neither total, rather than guessed. --json prints the\nrows and the summary as one object. See issue #128's lane for the full","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
